\id LAM - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.6
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Lamentations
\toc1 Sorrows
\toc2 Lamentations
\mt1 Sorrows
\mt3 commonly called
\mt2 Lamentations
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Jerusalem’s sorrows
\qa \bd א\bd* (Alef)
\q1
\v 1 Oh dear, how she sits alone, that city, a mighty people.
\q1 \add Once\add* powerful among the nations, she’s become like a widow.
\q1 \add From being\add* a princess among the provinces, she became a slave.
\qa \bd ב\bd* (Beyt)
\q1
\v 2 She weeps excessively in the night, and her tears are on her cheeks.
\q1 Despite having many lovers, no one brings her comfort.
\q1 All her friends betrayed her—they’ve become her enemies \add instead\add*.
\qa \bd ג\bd* (Gimel)
\q1
\v 3 Judah is exiled \add and made to experience\add* suffering and harsh slavery.
\q1 She sits among the nations \add but\add* finds no rest.
\q1 All who pursue her, overtake her even in the times of distress.
\qa \bd ד\bd* (Dalet)
\q1
\v 4 The roads of Zion mourn—no one’s coming to the scheduled festivals.
\q1 All her gates are desolate. Her priests are groaning.
\q1 Her young women are grieving and she makes herself bitter.
\qa \bd ה\bd* (He)
\q1
\v 5 Her enemies have become her masters—her enemies prosper
\q1 because Yahweh has made her suffer due to her frequent disobedience.
\q1 Her children get taken away as captives by the enemy.
\qa \bd ו\bd* (Vav)
\q1
\v 6 All her splendour was sent out from the daughter that’s Zion.
\q1 Her leaders have become like deer—they can’t find a pasture
\q1 so they have very little strength as they flee from the pursuer.
\qa \bd ז\bd* (Zayin)
\q1
\v 7 Jerusalem remembers the days of her suffering and her homelessness,
\q1 all her treasures which were from former days.
\q1 When her people fell into the hand of the enemy and there was no one to help her,
\q1 her enemies saw her—they laughed over her destruction.
\qa \bd ח\bd* (Het)
\q1
\v 8 Jerusalem disobeyed terribly, therefore, she became filth.
\q1 All her glory is scorned, because they’ve seen her nakedness.
\q1 Again, she groans and turns away.
\qa \bd ט\bd* (Tet)
\q1
\v 9 Her uncleanness is in her skirt.
\q1 She didn’t remember her end and her descent was marvellous.
\q1 There wasn’t any comforter for her.
\q1 “Oh, Yahweh, see my suffering, because the enemy is powerful.”
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\q1
\v 10 The enemy spread his hand over all her treasures.
\q1 So, she saw the nations; they entered her sanctuary—
\q1 those who you had forbidden to enter into your gathering place.
\qa \bd כ\bd* (Kaf)
\q1
\v 11 All her people are groaning, searching for bread.
\q1 They give their treasures to restore life with food.
\q1 “Look and see, Yahweh, because I’ve become worthless.
\qa \bd ל\bd* (Lamed)
\q1
\v 12 Is it nothing to you, all those going past on the road?
\q1 Look and see if there is any sorrow like my sorrow, which has been forced on me,
\q1 which Yahweh inflicted on the day of his fierce anger.
\qa \bd מ\bd* (Mem)
\q1
\v 13 He sent fire down from on high into my bones.
\q1 He spread a net for my feet and turned me away.
\q1 He made me desolate—feeling faint every day.
\qa \bd נ\bd* (Nun)
\q1
\v 14 The yoke of my disobedience is bound.
\q1 With his hand it’s assembled together and placed on my neck.
\q1 My strength is \add gone so now I\add* stumble.
\q1 My master arranged for me to be captured and I couldn’t defend myself.
\qa \bd ס\bd* (Samek)
\q1
\v 15 My master has rejected all my mighty warriors protecting me.
\q1 He has told us exactly when he plans to destroy my young men.
\q1 My master has stomped on the grapes for Judah’s virgin daughter.
\qa \bd ע\bd* (Ayin)
\q1
\v 16 As a result of those things, I weep.
\q1 My eyes, oh dear, my eyes overflow with water
\q1 because the comforter is far from me—one who restores my life.
\q1 My sons have become desolate, because the enemy is made mighty.”
\qa \bd פ\bd* (Pe)
\q1
\v 17 Zion has spread her hands wide—there is no comforter for her.
\q1 Yahweh commanded that those surrounding Jacob become his enemies.
\q1 Jerusalem has become filth in the middle of them.
\qa \bd צ\bd* (Tsade)
\q1
\v 18 “Yahweh is surely righteous, but I disobeyed his command.
\q1 Hear, all peoples, and see my sorrow.
\q1 My young men and women have been taken into captivity.
\qa \bd ק\bd* (Qof)
\q1
\v 19 I called to my lovers \add but\add* they deceived me.
\q1 My priests and my elders perished in the city,
\q1 while they searched for food to stay alive.
\qa \bd ר\bd* (Resh)
\q1
\v 20 Look, Yahweh, because I’m engulfed—my stomach churns.
\q1 My heart is undone within me, because I’ve disobeyed grievously.
\q1 Out in the street, the sword bereaves.
\q1 Inside the house, there’s death.
\qa \bd ש\bd* (Sin/Shin)
\q1
\v 21 Take notice because I’m groaning.
\q1 There’s no comforter for me.
\q1 All my enemies heard my trouble—they rejoiced that you did it.
\q1 You brought about the day \add of punishment\add* that you declared,
\q1 \add yet may those persecutors\add* become like me.
\qa \bd ת\bd* (Tav)
\q1
\v 22 May all their evil be noticed by you,
\q1 and deal with them just as you have dealt with me because of all my disobedience,
\q1 because my groans are many and my heart is faint.”
\c 2
\s1 Yahweh’s punishment of Jerusalem
\qa \bd א\bd* (Alef)
\q1
\v 1 Oh dear, how my master engulfed the daughter that’s Zion in his anger.
\q1 He’s thrown Israel’s splendour down from heaven to earth.
\q1 And he hasn’t remembered the footstool of his feet on the day of his anger.
\qa \bd ב\bd* (Beyt)
\q1
\v 2 My master devoured—he didn’t show compassion on all the pastures of Jacob.
\q1 In his fury, he knocked down the fortifications of the daughter of Judah.
\q1 He struck the earth with disgrace—the kingdom and its leaders.
\qa \bd ג\bd* (Gimel)
\q1
\v 3 With fierce anger he cut off all of Israel’s power.
\q1 He stopped using his strength to protect her from the enemy.
\q1 And he has burnt Jacob—it consumed the surroundings like a fire.
\qa \bd ד\bd* (Dalet)
\q1
\v 4 He’s bent his bow like an enemy.
\q1 His sword hand is posed like an adversary.
\q1 And he has slain all those precious to the eye in the tent of the daughter that’s Zion.
\q1 He has poured out his rage like fire.
\qa \bd ה\bd* (He)
\q1
\v 5 My master has become like an enemy—he’s swallowed up Israel.
\q1 He has swallowed up all her palaces—he’s destroyed its fortifications.
\q1 And he has multiplied mourning and lamentation within the daughter of Judah.
\qa \bd ו\bd* (Vav)
\q1
\v 6 And he was violent with his garden tent—he ruined his appointed place.
\q1 Yahweh has caused the appointed festival and Sabbath to be forgotten in Zion.
\q1 And he has despised the king and priest in his indignant anger.
\qa \bd ז\bd* (Zayin)
\q1
\v 7 My master has rejected his altar—he abandoned his sanctuary.
\q1 He has allowed the enemy \add to enter via\add* the walls of her palaces.
\q1 They raised a shout in Yahweh’s house, like on an appointed festival day.
\qa \bd ח\bd* (Het)
\q1
\v 8 Yahweh planned to destroy the wall of the daughter that’s Zion.
\q1 He stretched out the measuring line.
\q1 He didn’t withhold his hand from devouring,
\q1 and he’s brought the ramparts and wall to mourning. Together they wasted away.
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\q1
\v 9 Her gates sank into the ground.
\q1 He has destroyed and shattered their \add locking\add* bars.
\q1 Her king and her leaders are among the nations. There’s no law.
\q1 Also, her prophets don’t receive any visions from Yahweh.
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\q1
\v 10 They sit on the ground.
\q1 The elders of the daughter that’s Zion are silenced.
\q1 They have thrown up dust on their heads and put on \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth.
\q1 Jerusalem’s young women have bowed their heads to the ground.
\qa \bd כ\bd* (Kaf)
\q1
\v 11 My eyes have failed with tears.
\q1 My stomach churns.
\q1 My inner parts are poured out to the ground, because of the destruction of the daughter of my people,
\q1 when child and infant faint in the streets of the town.
\qa \bd ל\bd* (Lamed)
\q1
\v 12 They ask their mothers, “Where’s our food and drink?”
\q1 when they faint like the slain in the streets of the city,
\q1 when they die slowly in their mothers’ laps.
\qa \bd מ\bd* (Mem)
\q1
\v 13 What can I testify to you?
\q1 To what can I compare you, daughter of Jerusalem?
\q1 To what can I liken you, that I may comfort you, virgin daughter that’s Zion?
\q1 Because your brokenness is as great as the sea.
\q1 Who can heal you?
\qa \bd נ\bd* (Nun)
\q1
\v 14 Your prophets have ‘seen’ false and worthless visions for you,
\q1 and they didn’t expose your disobedience, to restore your fortunes,
\q1 ^but they have ‘received’ false and enticing messages for you.
\qa \bd ס\bd* (Samek)
\q1
\v 15 All those passing on the road clap their hands at you.
\q1 They hiss and shake their heads at the daughter of Jerusalem.
\q1 “Is this the city that they called ‘the fullness of beauty that makes the whole world happy’?”
\qa \bd ע\bd* (Ayin)
\q1
\v 16 All your enemies spoke out against you.
\q1 They hiss and grind their teeth.
\q1 They say, “We have devoured.
\q1 Surely this is the time we’d longed for.
\q1 We have found, we have seen.”
\qa \bd פ\bd* (Pe)
\q1
\v 17 Yahweh has done what he planned.
\q1 He’s fulfilled the message that he commanded in ancient times.
\q1 He has overthrown and shown no compassion,
\q1 and the enemy rejoiced over you.
\q1 He has enabled your enemies to get stronger.
\qa \bd צ\bd* (Tsade)
\q1
\v 18 Their heart cried out to my master,
\q1 “You walls of the daughter that’s Zion.”
\q1 Like a tearful river, let your tears flow down day and night.
\q1 Don’t allow yourself any relief from it.
\q1 Don’t rest your eyes \add from the tears\add*.
\qa \bd ק\bd* (Qof)
\q1
\v 19 Arise, shout in the night at the beginning of the night watches.
\q1 Pour out your heart like water in front of my master.
\q1 Lift up your hands to him for the life of your children who are fainting with hunger at the end of every street.
\qa \bd ר\bd* (Resh)
\q1
\v 20 Look, Yahweh, and see those you have dealt with in this way.
\q1 Should women eat their own newborn children?
\q1 Should priests and prophets be slaughtered in my master’s sanctuary?
\qa \bd ש\bd* (Sin/Shin)
\q1
\v 21 The young and the old lie down in the dirt in the streets.
\q1 My young men and women have fallen by the sword.
\q1 You’ve \add had them\add* struck down—at the time of your anger you slaughtered.
\q1 You showed no compassion.
\qa \bd ת\bd* (Tav)
\q1
\v 22 You’ve summoned terrors to surround me like summoning people to a festival day,
\q1 and there wasn’t anyone who escaped or survived on the day of Yahweh’s anger.
\q1 Those who I birthed and multiplied, my enemy has ended.
\c 3
\s1 Yahweh’s love
\qa \bd א\bd* (Alef)
\q1
\v 1 I am the man made to suffer from the rod of his fury.
\q1
\v 2 He led me and took me into darkness—not light.
\q1
\v 3 He certainly turned against me—he put the screws on me all day.
\qa \bd ב\bd* (Beyt)
\q1
\v 4 He made my skin and my flesh waste away.
\q1 He shattered my bones.
\q1
\v 5 He built \add a ramp\add* up against me
\q1 and besieged me with bitterness and hardship.
\q1
\v 6 He makes me live in darkness
\q1 like the dead of long ago.
\qa \bd ג\bd* (Gimel)
\q1
\v 7 He built a wall around me so I can’t escape.
\q1 He made my chains heavy.
\q1
\v 8 Even though I call out and cry for help,
\q1 he shuts out my prayer.
\q1
\v 9 He made roadblocks with \add large\add* cut stones.
\q1 He made it difficult to get anywhere.
\qa \bd ד\bd* (Dalet)
\q1
\v 10 He’s a bear lying in wait for me—
\q1 a lion hiding \add ready to attack\add*.
\q1
\v 11 He obstructed my paths and tore me apart.
\q1 He’s isolated me.
\q1
\v 12 He bent his bow \add back\add*
\q1 and set me up like a target for an arrow.
\qa \bd ה\bd* (He)
\q1
\v 13 He pierced my kidneys
\q1 with the arrows from his quiver.
\q1
\v 14 I became a joke to all the people \add groups\add*—
\q1 the \add one\add* they mocked all day \add long\add*.
\q1
\v 15 He filled me with bitterness.
\q1 He stuffed me with bitter herbs.
\qa \bd ו\bd* (Vav)
\q1
\v 16 He made my teeth grind with gravel.
\q1 He made me cower in the dust.
\q1
\v 17 \add Now\add* I can’t find any inner peace.
\q1 I’ve forgotten what prosperity is.
\q1
\v 18 Then I said, “My endurance has come to an end,
\q1 as has my hope in Yahweh.”
\qa \bd ז\bd* (Zayin)
\q1
\v 19 Remember my suffering and my wanderings,
\q1 the wormwood and bitterness.
\q1
\v 20 It continually remembers
\q1 and my insides turn to butter.
\q1
\v 21 I bring that to mind,
\q1 →therefore I have hope.
\qa \bd ח\bd* (Het)
\q1
\v 22 Yahweh’s loyal commitment doesn’t cease.
\q1 His mercy doesn’t end.
\q1
\v 23 His loyal commitment and his mercy are new morning by morning.
\q1 He is very faithful.
\q1
\v 24 “Yahweh is my portion,” I told myself,
\q1 →“therefore, I’ll place my hope in him.”
\qa \bd ט\bd* (Tet)
\q1
\v 25 Yahweh is good to those who depend on him—
\q1 to anyone sincerely wanting to follow him.
\q1
\v 26 It is good to be silent and wait
\q1 for Yahweh to rescue us.
\q2
\v 27 It’s good for people to experience hardship while they’re young.
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\q1
\v 28 A person should sit alone and stay quiet
\q1 when they’ve been disciplined \add by Yahweh\add*.
\q1
\v 29 Let him put his mouth in the dust—
\q1 perhaps there’s hope.
\q1
\v 30 Let him offer his cheek to the person striking him.
\q1 Let him be filled with shame.
\qa \bd כ\bd* (Kaf)
\q1
\v 31 My master won’t reject \add his people\add* forever.
\q1
\v 32 Though he brings grief,
\q1 he will be merciful due to the strength of his loyal commitment,
\q1
\v 33 because he doesn’t enjoy making people suffer
\q1 or delight in making humanity grieve.
\qa \bd ל\bd* (Lamed)
\q1
\v 34 Crushing all the prisoners of the earth under his feet,
\q1
\v 35 denying a person justice before the highest one,
\q1
\v 36 subverting a person’s lawsuit—the master approves none of those.
\qa \bd מ\bd* (Mem)
\q1
\v 37 Who said that and made it happen, if it wasn’t my master?
\q1
\v 38 Didn’t \add both\add* trouble and good come from the command of the highest one?
\q1
\v 39 How can any living person complain when it’s their disobedience that they’re getting punished for?
\qa \bd נ\bd* (Nun)
\q1
\v 40 Let’s examine our behaviour
\q1 and \add then\add* strive to return to Yahweh.
\q1
\v 41 Let us lift up our hearts
\q1 with our hands lifted to God in heaven.
\q1
\v 42 We’ve certainly broken your rules and disobeyed you.
\q1 You haven’t forgiven \add us\add*.
\qa \bd ס\bd* (Samek)
\q1
\v 43 You wrapped yourself with anger and chased after us.
\q1 You killed without showing any pity.
\q1
\v 44 You enveloped yourself in a cloud,
\q1 →so that no prayers could get through.
\q1
\v 45 You cause us to be considered as outcasts
\q1 and as garbage among the nations.
\qa \bd ע\bd* (Ayin)
\q1
\v 46 All our enemies spoke out against us.
\q1
\v 47 Panic and pitfall have come for us—
\q1 devastation and destruction.
\q1
\v 48 My eyes run with streams of water
\q1 because my people have been destroyed.
\qa \bd פ\bd* (Pe)
\q1
\v 49 My tears flowed continually without any relief,
\q1
\v 50 until Yahweh looked down from heaven and saw.
\q1
\v 51 What I see grieves my insides,
\q1 because of \add the suffering\add* of the city’s daughters.
\qa \bd צ\bd* (Tsade)
\q1
\v 52 My enemies certainly hunted me without cause—
\q1 like hunting a bird.
\q1
\v 53 They shut down my life by throwing me into a pit,
\q1 and \add then\add* heaving a rock onto the top.
\q1
\v 54 They made water flow over my head.
\q1 I said, ‘This must be my end!’
\qa \bd ק\bd* (Qof)
\q1
\v 55 I called out your name, Yahweh, from the depths of the pit.
\q1
\v 56 You heard my voice.
\q1 ‘Don’t ignore my cry for help
\q1 \add and my need for\add* relief.’
\q1
\v 57 You approached \add me\add* when I called you
\q1 \add and\add* told me not to be afraid.
\qa \bd ר\bd* (Resh)
\q1
\v 58 My master, you helped with my internal conflict.
\q1 You paid out to save my life.
\q1
\v 59 Yes, Yahweh, you saw my suffering.
\q1 Judge my case.
\q1
\v 60 You’ve seen all their insults—
\q1 all their plots against me.
\qa \bd ש\bd* (Sin/Shin)
\q1
\v 61 You’ve heard their scorn, Yahweh—
\q1 all their plots against me.
\q1
\v 62 Those harassing me spend all day speaking out against me
\q1 and \add singing\add* songs against me.
\q1
\v 63 See how, whether they’re sitting or rising,
\q1 \add they have\add* their mocking song.
\qa \bd ת\bd* (Tav)
\q1
\v 64 You, Yahweh, will pay them back for what they’ve done.
\q1
\v 65 Give them over to shamelessness.
\q1 May your curse be on them.
\q1
\v 66 Chase after them in your anger,
\q1 and wipe them off the face of the earth, Yahweh.
\c 4
\s1 Zion’s punishment
\qa \bd א\bd* (Alef)
\q1
\v 1 Wow, how the gold is tarnished—the pure gold has changed.
\q1 The holy stones are scattered on the corners of every street.
\qa \bd ב\bd* (Beyt)
\q1
\v 2 The precious sons of Zion were once comparable with fine gold.
\q1 Now they’re regarded as clay jars—the work of a potter’s hands.
\qa \bd ג\bd* (Gimel)
\q1
\v 3 Even the jackals offer their teats to suckle their cubs,
\q1 ^\add but\add* my people are fierce like the ostriches in the wilderness.
\qa \bd ד\bd* (Dalet)
\q1
\v 4 The tongue of the breast-feeding baby sticks to the roof of his mouth with thirst.
\q1 The children ask for food from those distributing \add it, but\add* there’s none for them.
\qa \bd ה\bd* (He)
\q1
\v 5 Those \add used to\add* eating the finest food are \add now\add* desolate in the streets.
\q1 Those \add once\add* attended by \add servants wearing\add* scarlet \add now\add* embrace piles of rubbish.
\qa \bd ו\bd* (Vav)
\q1
\v 6 The guilt of my people is greater than Sodom’s sin.
\q1 That city was overthrown in an instant, but no one shed any tears about it.
\qa \bd ז\bd* (Zayin)
\q1
\v 7 Her religious leaders were purer than snow—whiter than milk.
\q1 Their bones were redder than coral—their parts were sapphire.
\qa \bd ח\bd* (Het)
\q1
\v 8 Blackness has darkened their form—they’re not \add even\add* recognised in the streets.
\q1 Their skin has shrivelled on their bones—it’s become as dry and stiff as bark.
\qa \bd ט\bd* (Tet)
\q1
\v 9 Those slain by the sword were happier than those who died from hunger—
\q1 those who slowly wasted away as if they were pierced through by the lack of produce from the countryside.
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\q1
\v 10 The hands of compassionate women boiled their children.
\q1 They became a meal for them during the destruction of my people.
\qa \bd כ\bd* (Kaf)
\q1
\v 11 Yahweh brought his rage to completion.
\q1 He poured out his fierce anger
\q1 and he kindled a fire in Zion that consumed its foundations.
\qa \bd ל\bd* (Lamed)
\q1
\v 12 None of the kings of the earth, and none of the world’s inhabitants, \add ever\add* believed
\q1 that the adversary and enemy would get through Jerusalem’s gates.
\qa \bd מ\bd* (Mem)
\q1
\v 13 \add However,\add* the sins of her prophets were \add even\add* more than the disobedience of her priests.
\q1 They had poured the blood of godly people out \add right there\add* in the middle of the city.
\qa \bd נ\bd* (Nun)
\q1
\v 14 They stumble blind in the streets.
\q1 They’re defiled with blood.
\q1 No one \add even\add* dares to touch their clothes.
\qa \bd ס\bd* (Samek)
\q1
\v 15 “Away! Unclean!” they shouted at them. “Away! Away! Don’t touch!”
\q1 →So they fled \add Israel\add*, wandering around,
\q1 ^\add but\add* it was said among the nations, “They can’t stay here any longer.”
\qa \bd ע\bd* (Ayin)
\q1
\v 16 Yahweh’s presence scattered them—he won’t continue to watch over them.
\q1 \add The people\add* no longer honoured the priests.
\q1 They showed no favour to the elders.
\qa \bd פ\bd* (Pe)
\q1
\v 17 Our eyes failed as we waited uselessly for help.
\q1 From our watchtowers, we expected to see \add other\add* nations come to rescue us, but no.
\qa \bd צ\bd* (Tsade)
\q1
\v 18 They tracked our steps,
\q2 walking in our paths.
\q1 Our end was coming closer.
\q1 Our days had been numbered, because our end had come.
\qa \bd ק\bd* (Qof)
\q1
\v 19 The ones chasing after us were faster than the eagles in the sky.
\q1 They chased us over the mountains,
\q1 \add then\add* they ambushed us in the wilderness.
\qa \bd ר\bd* (Resh)
\q1
\v 20 A breath of our nostrils, Yahweh’s anointed one was captured in their pits—
\q1 the one we’d spoken about, \add saying\add*, “We’ll live among the nations under his protection.”
\qa \bd ש\bd* (Sin/Shin)
\q1
\v 21 Celebrate and be glad, Edom, living there in the Uz region.
\q1 The cup \add of suffering\add* will also pass to you.
\q1 You’ll get drunk and go around naked.
\qa \bd ת\bd* (Tav)
\q1
\v 22 People of Zion, your guilt will finish—he won’t extend your exile.
\q1 ^He’ll deal with your guilt, people of Edom—he’ll uncover your sins.
\c 5
\s1 The mercy of Israel
\q1
\v 1 Yahweh, take notice of what’s happened to us.
\q1 Look and see our disgrace.
\b
\q1
\v 2 \add The land that was\add* our inheritance has been turned over to strangers—
\q1 our houses to foreigners.
\b
\q1
\v 3 We have become orphans, without a father,
\q1 and our mothers are like widows.
\b
\q1
\v 4 We pay to drink water.
\q1 We bring in our \add fire\add*wood for a price.
\b
\q1
\v 5 We’re driven hard \add with the yoke\add* on our necks.
\q1 We work hard, yet never get any rest.
\b
\q1
\v 6 We’ve made a treaty with Egypt,
\q1 \add and with\add* Assyria to get food.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Our ancestors sinned but they’re gone now.
\q1 →We ourselves bear \add the results of\add* their disobedience.
\b
\q1
\v 8 \add Babylonian\add* slaves rule over us.
\q1 There’s no one to rescue us from their control.
\b
\q1
\v 9 We bring in our food at the cost of our lives,
\q1 due to the sword blades \add out there\add* in the wilderness.
\b
\q1
\v 10 Our skin grows as hot as an oven
\q1 from the burning heat of hunger.
\b
\q1
\v 11 Women are raped in Zion—
\q1 young women in Judah’s cities.
\b
\q1
\v 12 Princes were hung by their hands.
\q1 The \add city\add* elders were treated with no respect.
\b
\q1
\v 13 Young men lift the grind stone
\q1 and boys stagger \add under loads of\add* wood.
\b
\q1
\v 14 The elders no longer make decisions at the \add city\add* gate,
\q1 The young men have stopped their music.
\b
\q1
\v 15 We’re no longer happy and contented.
\q1 Our dancing has turned to mourning.
\b
\q1
\v 16 The crown has fallen off our head.
\q1 We won’t end well, because we’ve sinned.
\b
\q1
\v 17 As a result of that, we’ve become depressed.
\q1 On account of those things, our eyes grow dim.
\b
\q1
\v 18 On account of Mt. Zion,
\q1 which is desolate with jackals roaming over it.
\b
\q1
\v 19 You, Yahweh, exist forever.
\q1 You’ll sit on your throne throughout the generations.
\b
\q1
\v 20 Why do you forget us for so long?
\q1 Why do you abandon us for many days?
\b
\q1
\v 21 Bring us back to yourself, Yahweh, and we’ll be restored.
\q1 Renew our life back to what it was before
\q1
\v 22 unless you’ve totally rejected us—
\q1 unless you’re angry with us beyond measure.
